From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 17:02:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS: never remove exec intervals for a bulk non-exec range
 (CEF NOEXEC wall)

PartitionAlloc RESERVING its 16GB soft pool was wiping the executable
interval of every module inside it. HandleMemoryProtectionNotification
treats any non-exec notification as "this range is no longer executable"
and removes it from XIntervals, so one call covering
0x7000000000-0x7400000000 erased libcef's .text. The decoder then reported
NOEXEC at libcef code addresses, raised FAULT_SIGSEGV, and killed
webhelper threads -- 42 SEGV loop fatals in the run before this fix.

The notification arrives via NotifyMemoryAlloc, NOT NotifyMemoryProtect,
which is why guarding Wine's NtProtectVirtualMemory never fired: nothing
was protecting anything. prot=0x1 (PAGE_NOACCESS) on the observed call
confirms a pure reservation. The guard belongs at the single removal
choke point all three callers share.

Only the removal branch is guarded, so the insert path and DEP promotion
are untouched. Modules keep their own mappings and issue their own
per-section notifications, so ignoring a bulk range cannot lose a genuine
executability transition.

Measured on device: 4 PartitionAlloc pools land with zero interval loss;
SEGV loop fatals 42 -> 0, iOS-noexec 3 -> 0, xquery misses 6 -> 0.
libcef now executes deep into its own initialisation.

Also here: route FEX LogMan through __wine_dbg_output. FEX's iOS path used
::write(2,...), but xtajit64 is a PE, so fd 2 is the CRT's rather than the
unix fd the app redirects -- all FEX logging was silently discarded. This
is what made the above diagnosable at all.

Plus NotifyMapViewOfSection ThreadState guard relaxation with pending-map
replay, and the probes used to find this ([iOS-xins]/[iOS-xrem]/
[iOS-xquery]/[iOS-noexec]).

